<!-- eSIM Card vs UPeSIM Comparison Content -->
<div class="content-section">

  {% set esimcardusPrimary = providerA.codes | findCode(providerA.primaryCode) %}
  {% set upesimPrimary = providerB.codes | findCode(providerB.primaryCode) %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>eSIM Card</strong> if you want a plan sized exactly to your trip, down to the day, and don't mind picking from dozens of options to get there. Pick <strong>UPeSIM</strong> if you'd rather install an eSIM weeks before you fly and forget about it until you land. Both are budget-leaning, both hand out a public 10% code, and neither offers a real refund once you've used the data.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Dozens of Sized Tiers vs a Plan That Waits for You</h2>
  <p>
    eSIM Card's United States page alone lists roughly 30 combinations: seven fixed-data tiers from 1GB to 100GB, each sold at several validity windows from 3 to 90 days, plus two unlimited tiers ("Unlimited" and "Unlimited Plus") running 1 to 30 days. UPeSIM, a French MVNO running since 2018, sells a simpler lineup of fixed prepaid blocks with no auto-renewal, covering 200+ countries and territories through local carrier partnerships.
  </p>
  <p>
    The bigger practical difference is when the clock starts. UPeSIM's own policy is that a plan doesn't begin counting down until your phone actually connects to the destination network, not when you install the eSIM or scan the QR code, so it's built to be set up at home well before departure. The catch: UPeSIM's own FAQ says a profile that's installed but never activated goes stale and shows as expired after 60 days, so it's only good for trips within about two months. eSIM Card's own materials don't publish an equivalent activation-timing policy, so we can't say installing one ahead of time behaves the same way there; if you're buying eSIM Card, install it closer to your travel dates rather than assuming it'll wait.
  </p>

